      "solution": "import math\n\ndef chernoff_poisson(lambda_, k):\n    if lambda_ == 0:\n        return 0.0\n    if k <= lambda_:\n        return 1.0\n\n    delta = k / lambda_ - 1.0\n\n    log_term = delta - (1.0 + delta) * math.log(1.0 + delta)\n    return math.exp(lambda_ * log_term)\n\n# Updated to use lambdaUSER to match your stub generator\nn, lambda_u, C, L = input().split()\nn = int(n)\nlambda_u = float(lambda_u)\nC = int(C)\nL = float(L)\n\nlam = n * lambda_u\n\nleft, right = 0, 1\n\nwhile chernoff_poisson(lam, C + right) > L:\n    right *= 2\n\nwhile left < right:\n    mid = (left + right) // 2\n    if chernoff_poisson(lam, C + mid) <= L:\n        right = mid\n    else:\n        left = mid + 1\n\nprint(left)",
      "statement": "<<Goal>>\n\nA network router receives traffic from multiple users. Packet arrivals follow a Poisson process, and overload probabilities are evaluated using a Chernoff bound approximation.\n\nThe router can transmit up to [[C]] packets per time slot and has a buffer of size [[B]]. Determine the minimum buffer size required to ensure that packet loss probability stays below [[L]].",
